{
  "text": "No concrete defect. The plan and the patch are sound, and nothing in the diff requires a code change.\n\nThe scope is right. Shiki stays on JavaScript because no WASM migration qualified. The private `ax-code-diff` pilot only does duplicate comparison, so leaving it out of diff preview and hunks is correct. The patch stays inside the three intended edits: skip marked’s inline lex, put the incremental cache in lexer coordinates, and read the last fence line without splitting the block.\n\n**Inline lex.** `new marked.Lexer().blockTokens(...)` is the block phase that `Lexer.lex` runs after newline normalization. A new lexer each call keeps the undrained inline queue on that object, so it cannot affect the next tick. This file only reads `type` and `raw`; remark parses inline syntax later. Block `raw` is fixed before inline tokens are filled, so skipping that drain does not move block boundaries. The `inlineTokens` spy matches the current marked 17 behavior.\n\n**CRLF coordinates.** `/\\r\\n?/g` → `\\n` matches `Lexer.lex` (`\\r\\n` or a lone `\\r`). The cache stores that normalized string, and `stablePrefixLength` is a sum of token `raw` lengths in the same space. Append-only updates stay prefix-stable, including a `\\r` at the end of one chunk and `\\n` at the start of the next: `\\r` and `\\r\\n` both become one `\\n`, so the new normalized text still starts with the cached text. Re-lexing `normalizedText.slice(stablePrefixLength)` cannot repeat a completed block because of a longer CRLF prefix. An older in-memory cache that still holds CRLF fails `startsWith` and is re-lexed from scratch.\n\n**Fence line.** After `trimEnd()`, `lastIndexOf(\"\\n\")` plus `slice` is the same last line as `split(\"\\n\").at(-1)`, including a string with no newline (`lastIndexOf` is `-1`, so `slice(0)` is the whole line). `.trim()` and the closer regex are unchanged.\n\n**Failure and reference-definition paths.** Both still return one whole-text block and `emptyCache` on the original string. Reuse requires `stablePrefixLength > 0`, so that un-normalized cache is not sliced in the normalized coordinate space. A later successful lex simply starts over. Malformed input that throws inside `blockTokens` still hits that fallback.\n\nThe new tests cover the real risks: normalized offsets with reused block identity, `\\n` / `\\r\\n` / `\\r` raws against `marked.lexer`, a CRLF pair split across chunks, and the inline spy. Three pre-fix failures and 59 passing focused tests fit that picture.\n\nDo not treat this as a measured speedup. The chat parser is still dominated by remark; the win here is removing a second inline tokenization and correcting cache coordinates.\n\nWatch items only, not fixes: the spy is tied to `Lexer.prototype.inlineTokens`, so a future marked that inlines through another method could do extra work while the test stays green; whole-block fallback raw can still contain CRLF while lexed blocks are LF, which is the same split `marked.lexer` already had.",
  "stopReason": "end_turn",
  "sessionId": "01a1a0b7-0c77-7171-9054-85e0ea84cc11",
